  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAnother warm and dry day is on display with current temperatures\nspanning the 80s as a 586 dam ridge remains over the middle\nMississippi Valley. Late tonight into early tomorrow, eyes turn\nto the remnants of Hurricane Isaias. After making forecast\nlandfall along Alabama and Florida Coast as a Category\n3 hurricane, Isaias is forecast to begin making a northeastern\ntrek inland.\n\nThe primary impacts with Isaias' remnants here in the Mid-South\nwill be gusty winds and periods of heavy rainfall. The latest\nforecast from the NHC does have Isaias impacting portions of the\nMid-South at Tropical Storm strength. Though there still exists\nuncertainty with the overall impacts, storm track, and intensity\nthis far inland, four of our counties (Lee, Itawamba, Chickasaw,\nand Monroe) are now in a Tropical Storm Warning through tomorrow \nafternoon with wind gusts in excess of 40 mph in these areas. The \nrest of the Mid-South is now under a Wind Advisory through \ntomorrow for sustained winds up to 25 mph and gusts up to 45 mph. \nThe strongest winds are anticipated in areas east of the \nMississippi River, though, areas west will still be fairly gusty.\n\nCurrent upper-level flow and a few shortwaves could steer this\nsystem further east or further west. Pending its track will\nimpact the amount of rain we receive as its parent low begins to\nocclude along the eastern periphery of the Mid-South. Latest WPC\nguidance has backed off with rainfall amounts east of the\nMississippi River with these areas struggling to reach an inch of\ntotal precipitation. Whereas areas east of the Mississippi River\nare forecast to see up to 2.5\" of rainfall with locally higher\namounts, greatest over northeast Mississippi and along the\nTennessee River. The latest NBM guidance does have a 60-90% of\n>2\" and a 40-60% chance of amounts >3\". A Flood Watch will go\ninto effect early tomorrow morning and remain through sunset for\nmany areas east of the Mississippi River as forecast PWATs begin\nto surge near the 90th percentile in these areas. One thing to\nnote, this tropical (stratiform) rain will be efficient despite\nthe quick movement of Isaias.\n\nBy mid-morning Sunday, Isaias' remnants will have exited our area\nleaving a pleasant day across the Mid-South with highs in the 70s\nand 80s. Behind Isaias, a warm and dry pattern will begin as\nupper-level northwest flow begins to quickly build across the\nMississippi Valley. We begin a warming trend as an upper-level\nridge starts to broaden over the Lower Mississippi Valley from\nthe western Gulf. Towards the end of the workweek, our next\nsystem looks to arrive potentially cooling us bringing our next\nshot at precipitation.\n\nAEH",
